// Living Environment Regents — August 2018
export default {
  id: 'li-aug-2018',
  subject: 'living-environment',
  year: 2018,
  session: 'August',
  totalMinutes: 180,
  questions: [
    { number: 1, part: 'A', text: 'Which human activity most directly causes a signiﬁcant increase in the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere?', choices: ['growing corn for food', 'not using products containing plastics', 'driving cars long distances', 'planting large numbers of trees'], topic: 'General', correct: 2, explanation: 'Cars burn fossil fuels (gasoline), releasing CO₂ stored in petroleum directly into the atmosphere. Long-distance driving burns large quantities of fuel, making it a significant direct source of atmospheric CO₂.', diveDeep: 'The combustion of fossil fuels (coal, oil, natural gas) releases ancient carbon stored over millions of years back into the atmosphere as CO₂ in a very short time, far exceeding natural carbon cycle rates. CO₂ is a greenhouse gas that traps heat. Growing corn involves photosynthesis which absorbs CO₂. Avoiding plastics reduces petrochemical production slightly. Planting trees actively removes CO₂. Driving is the most direct and significant CO₂ source among these options. On the Regents, burning fossil fuels (cars, power plants, factories) is always the primary human cause of atmospheric CO₂ increase and climate change.' },
    { number: 2, part: 'A', text: 'An immune response is primarily due to the body\'s white blood cells recognizing', choices: ['a hormone imbalance', 'abiotic organisms', 'foreign antigens', 'known antibiotics'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, explanation: 'White blood cells (lymphocytes and phagocytes) recognize foreign antigens — protein markers on the surface of pathogens, foreign cells, or transplanted organs — as non-self, triggering an immune response.', diveDeep: 'Antigens are molecular markers (usually proteins) on cell surfaces that the immune system uses to distinguish "self" from "non-self." When white blood cells detect foreign antigens (from bacteria, viruses, or transplanted tissue), they mount an immune response: B-cells produce antibodies that bind to the antigen; T-cells directly attack infected cells. Hormone imbalances are not what triggers white blood cell responses. Abiotic organisms do not exist — "abiotic" means non-living (no such thing as an abiotic organism). Antibiotics are medicines that fight bacteria, not immune triggers. On the Regents, immune response = white blood cells recognizing foreign antigens, then producing specific antibodies.' },
    { number: 3, part: 'A', text: 'In an effort to reduce the number of deaths due to malaria, scientists have successfully introduced a gene into mosquitoes. The gene makes the mosquitoes unable to support the development of the parasite that causes malaria. The technique used to produce this new variety of mosquito is most likely', choices: ['chromatography', 'genetic engineering', 'electrophoresis of genes', 'selective breeding'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 1, explanation: 'Introducing a specific gene from another organism into the mosquito\'s genome is the definition of genetic engineering — inserting foreign DNA into an organism to give it a new trait.', diveDeep: 'Genetic engineering (recombinant DNA technology) involves using restriction enzymes to cut DNA, inserting a desired gene, and introducing it into the target organism using a vector (like a plasmid). In this case, a gene preventing malaria parasite development is inserted into mosquito DNA. Chromatography separates molecules by chemical properties — it does not insert genes. Electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size — it does not introduce new genes. Selective breeding requires choosing and mating organisms with desired traits across many generations — it does not directly insert specific genes. On the Regents, any scenario involving inserting a specific gene into an organism = genetic engineering.' },
    { number: 4, part: 'A', text: 'The organic compounds that scientists use to cut, copy, and move segments of DNA are', choices: ['carbohydrates', 'hormones', 'enzymes', 'starches Base your answers to questions = and 6 on the diagram below and on your knowledge of biology. Rabbits Snakes Mice Trees Mountain lions Crickets Frogs Hawks Deer Grass'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 1 is "hormones," but the biological tools used to cut, copy, and manipulate DNA are restriction enzymes and polymerases — both are enzymes (index 2). Enzymes (restriction enzymes to cut DNA, ligases to join it, and polymerases to copy it) are the organic molecular tools used in genetic engineering and molecular biology to manipulate DNA segments.', diveDeep: 'Restriction endonucleases cut DNA at specific recognition sequences (palindromic base sequences). DNA polymerases copy DNA (as in PCR). DNA ligases join DNA fragments. All of these are proteins — specifically enzymes (biological catalysts). Carbohydrates and starches are energy molecules — they do not interact with DNA. Hormones are chemical messengers — they regulate cell processes but are not used as molecular tools for cutting or copying DNA. On the Regents, "cut, copy, and move DNA" is a direct description of enzyme function in biotechnology. This is a frequently tested biotechnology concept connecting molecular tools to genetic engineering.' },
    { number: 5, part: 'A', text: 'Which statement most accurately predicts the result of interfering with populations in the web?', choices: ['Removing the cricket population will have little effect on the balance of the food web.', 'Removing all the mountain lions from the food web will beneﬁt the ecosystem.', 'Removing the cricket and rabbit popula- tions would cause the number of trees to decrease.', 'Removing deer from the food web will affect the rabbit and grass populations.'], topic: 'General', correct: 3, explanation: 'Deer eat grass and compete with rabbits for food. Removing deer would reduce competition, allowing the rabbit population to increase, which would consume more grass — affecting both the rabbit and grass populations through trophic cascade.', diveDeep: 'Food web interactions include predation, competition, and indirect effects. Deer eat grass (competing with rabbits for that resource). When deer are removed, grass is less grazed → rabbit populations increase (more food, less competition) → grass populations decrease from more rabbit grazing. Crickets are a significant food source for frogs and other consumers — removing them would significantly affect the web (choice A is wrong). Removing mountain lions would allow deer, rabbits, and others to overpopulate — harming, not benefiting, the ecosystem (choice B is wrong). Removing crickets and rabbits would reduce pressure on grass and trees — trees would likely increase, not decrease (choice C is wrong). On the Regents, food web removal questions require tracing all direct feeding connections.' },
    { number: 6, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/context_5_6.png', part: 'A', text: 'A factor not shown in the diagram that provides energy for living organisms is', choices: ['carbon dioxide', 'the Sun', 'water', 'oxygen Part A Answer all questions in this part. Directions (1–30): For each statement or question, record on the separate answer sheet the number of the word or expression that, of those given, best completes the statement or answers the question. Living Environment–Aug. \'18'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 2 is "water," but water does not provide energy. The Sun (index 1) is the ultimate energy source for all ecosystems through photosynthesis. The Sun is the original energy source not shown in food web diagrams — all energy in the food web ultimately comes from solar energy captured by producers through photosynthesis.', diveDeep: 'Food web diagrams show feeding relationships between organisms (biotic components) but typically do not show the Sun, which is the abiotic energy input driving the entire system. Producers (plants, algae) capture solar energy through photosynthesis and convert it to chemical energy (glucose). This energy flows through the food web at each trophic level. Carbon dioxide is a raw material for photosynthesis, not an energy source. Water is essential for life but does not provide energy directly. Oxygen is a byproduct of photosynthesis, not an energy source. On the Regents, the energy source not shown in food web diagrams is always the Sun.' },
    { number: 7, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists have found a gene that makes a protein called PKG that controls certain behaviors in many types of ants. The soldier ant will help collect food when it has a low level of PKG. When it has a high level of PKG, the soldier ant will protect and defend its colony. Soldier ants that are given PKG are more likely to ignore food sources and attack intruders. Which conclusion can best be made from this information?', choices: ['PKG protein is synthesized only by the soldier ants.', 'Genes control which type of amino acids a cell can make.', 'Eating too much protein makes some organisms very aggressive.', 'The behavior of soldier ants is controlled in part by the PKG protein.'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 3, explanation: 'The evidence shows that changing PKG levels directly changes ant behavior (food collection vs. defense) — demonstrating that the PKG protein plays a direct role in controlling soldier ant behavior.', diveDeep: 'This question illustrates the gene → protein → behavior connection: the PKG gene codes for the PKG protein, and the protein\'s concentration affects neural function and behavior. This concept is related to how hormones (which are proteins or derived from proteins) regulate behavior. The passage does not state that only soldier ants make PKG — it says PKG controls behaviors in many ant types (choice A is unsupported). Genes code for the sequence of amino acids in specific proteins, not which types of amino acids a cell can make in general (choice B is misleading). The experiment involved injecting PKG protein, not eating protein — the mechanism is specific (choice C is a distortion). On the Regents, gene expression → protein → phenotype/behavior is a key conceptual chain.' },
    { number: 8, part: 'A', text: 'Genetic researchers have discovered a number of different gene mutations that have led to the development of cancer. These mutations affect how frequently a cell reproduces. Which process would be directly inﬂuenced by these mutations?', choices: ['differentiation of cells in an embryo', 'meiotic cell division', 'division of sperm and egg cells', 'mitotic cell division'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 3, explanation: 'Cancer involves uncontrolled cell reproduction — specifically abnormal mitosis. Mutations that affect how frequently a cell reproduces directly influence mitotic cell division, which is the process by which somatic (body) cells divide.', diveDeep: 'Normal mitosis is tightly regulated by cell cycle checkpoints. Cancer occurs when mutations in genes that control these checkpoints (tumor suppressor genes like p53, or proto-oncogenes) allow cells to divide uncontrollably. This results in tumor formation. Differentiation is about cells developing specialized functions — while related to development, it is not the primary process affected by mutations causing uncontrolled division. Meiosis and sperm/egg division (choices B and C) occur in reproductive organs — cancer mutations typically affect somatic cells and mitosis. On the Regents, uncontrolled cell reproduction = abnormal mitosis = cancer. This concept connects genetics (mutations) to cell biology (mitosis).' },
    { number: 9, part: 'A', text: 'Lobsters are crustaceans related to crayﬁsh, crabs, and shrimp. Most lobsters are a reddish- brown color, but on rare occasions, they can be orange, blue, or even multicolored. These color differences can be caused by', choices: ['genetic variations', 'different numbers of offspring', 'overpopulation and excessive resources', 'the instability of the ecosystem'], topic: 'General', correct: 0, explanation: 'Color variations in lobsters result from genetic differences — different alleles controlling pigment production in their exoskeletons. These genetic variations arise from mutations and recombination during reproduction.', diveDeep: 'Coat/color variation within a species is a classic example of phenotypic variation driven by genotypic variation (different alleles at genes controlling pigment synthesis). Blue, orange, or multicolored lobsters have specific mutations in pigment genes that alter the color compounds in their shells. The number of offspring does not explain color variation. Overpopulation and resources affect population size, not individual color. Ecosystem instability affects populations, not individual coloration. On the Regents, variation in physical traits within a population = genetic variation = different alleles. This variation is the raw material on which natural selection acts.' },
    { number: 10, part: 'A', text: 'Which two factors could lead to the evolution of a species over time?', choices: ['overproduction of offspring and no variation', 'changes in the genes of body cells and extinction', 'struggle for survival and fossilization', 'changes in the genes of sex cells and survival of the ﬁttest'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 3, explanation: 'Evolution requires heritable genetic changes — which occur in sex cells (gametes) so they can be passed to offspring — combined with natural selection (survival of the fittest), which determines which genetic variants become more common.', diveDeep: 'Darwin\'s theory of evolution by natural selection requires: (1) heritable variation (changes in sex cell genes that get passed to offspring), (2) overproduction (more offspring than can survive), and (3) differential survival/reproduction (survival of the fittest). Body cell mutations (choice B) cannot be inherited by offspring. Overproduction alone without variation (choice A) cannot lead to evolution. Fossilization is a record of past life, not a mechanism of evolution (choice C). On the Regents, evolution = changes in inheritable genes (sex cells) + selection. Remember: somatic (body cell) mutations are not heritable; germ cell (sex cell) mutations are heritable and can drive evolution.' },
    { number: 11, part: 'A', text: 'As human red blood cells mature, they lose their nuclei. As a result of this loss, which process would be impossible for mature red blood cells to carry out?', choices: ['excretion', 'reproduction', 'respiration', 'transport'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 2 is "respiration," but mature red blood cells do carry out anaerobic respiration (glycolysis). The most impossible process without a nucleus is reproduction (cell division), since mitosis requires DNA replication directed by the nucleus. Without a nucleus (and therefore without DNA), mature red blood cells cannot reproduce — they cannot undergo mitosis because there is no genetic material to replicate and distribute to daughter cells.', diveDeep: 'The nucleus contains DNA and directs all cellular processes requiring genetic information, including cell division. Without a nucleus, a cell cannot replicate its DNA and therefore cannot divide (reproduce). This is why red blood cells cannot multiply — they must be constantly replaced by stem cells in bone marrow. Red blood cells can still excrete waste, perform gas transport (carrying O₂ via hemoglobin), and carry out some cellular respiration without a nucleus. On the Regents, loss of nucleus = cannot divide/reproduce is the direct logical connection. The nucleus is essential for cell division because mitosis requires DNA replication.' },
    { number: 12, part: 'A', text: 'To clone a mammal, a cloned embryo is often put into an adult female of the same species to continue internal development. The structure in which the embryo will develop is the', choices: ['ovary', 'uterus', 'placenta', 'egg'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 2 is "placenta," but the placenta is not where the embryo develops — it develops in the uterus (index 1). The embryo implants in the lining of the uterus, and the placenta forms as a support organ. The uterus is the organ in the female reproductive system where an embryo/fetus develops throughout gestation. Cloned embryos must be implanted into a uterus for development to continue.', diveDeep: 'Cloning (somatic cell nuclear transfer) involves replacing the nucleus of an egg cell with a nucleus from a somatic cell, stimulating it to develop into an embryo, then implanting it into a surrogate mother\'s uterus. The uterus provides the physical space, the uterine lining for implantation, and support through the placenta that forms later. Ovaries produce eggs and hormones (not where embryos develop). The placenta forms after implantation — it is a support structure, not the location of development. Eggs are fertilized in the fallopian tubes, not where ongoing development occurs. On the Regents, embryo development location = uterus.' },
    { number: 13, part: 'A', text: 'Nuclear power plants, which produce electrical energy, use large quantities of water for cooling. Often, small ﬁsh, larvae, and ﬁsh eggs are sucked in along with the cooling water and destroyed. This example illustrates how', choices: ['industrialization can have positive and negative effects', 'removal of these organisms has no effect on an ecosystem', 'direct harvesting increases the natural ﬁsh population', 'energy is generated without producing wastes'], topic: 'General', correct: 0, explanation: 'Nuclear power plants provide a positive benefit (electricity generation) but also cause negative ecological effects (killing fish larvae and eggs) — illustrating the dual positive and negative impacts of industrial technology.', diveDeep: 'Technology almost always involves tradeoffs: benefits and costs. Nuclear power provides low-carbon electricity (a significant advantage over fossil fuels for climate change) but has disadvantages including thermal pollution, disruption of aquatic life from water intake, and nuclear waste storage issues. The removal of fish larvae and eggs DOES affect ecosystems — it reduces fish populations and disrupts food webs (choice B is wrong). Killing fish is not "direct harvesting" in the traditional sense, and it decreases fish populations (choice C is wrong). Nuclear power plants do produce wastes (radioactive waste, heated water) (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, technology questions consistently lead to the answer: both positive and negative effects.' },
    { number: 14, part: 'A', text: 'Acid rain is a major problem in the Adirondack Mountains. Evidence that acid rain negatively affected the Adirondack ecosystem is that', choices: ['this rain has increased the amount of water in Adirondack lakes', 'there has been a decrease in the variety of ﬁsh found in Adirondack lakes', 'the amount of carbon dioxide in the air over the Adirondack Mountains has drastically decreased in recent years', 'the number of heterotrophic organisms in Adirondack lakes has increased'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, explanation: 'A decrease in the variety of fish species (reduced biodiversity) is direct evidence that acid rain has negatively impacted the ecosystem by killing acid-sensitive species and reducing overall species diversity.', diveDeep: 'Acid rain (caused by SO₂ and NOₓ emissions reacting with atmospheric water to form sulfuric and nitric acids) lowers the pH of lakes and streams. Most aquatic organisms, including fish, have narrow pH tolerance ranges — when pH drops below ~5.5, many fish species cannot survive or reproduce. The result is reduced fish diversity and population declines. More water in lakes (choice A) would not indicate harm. Decreased CO₂ (choice C) would actually be a benefit for reducing acidification. Increased heterotrophs (choice D) would suggest ecosystem health, not degradation. On the Regents, ecosystem damage is evidenced by decreased species diversity, reduced populations, or loss of specific sensitive species.' },
    { number: 15, part: 'A', text: 'Which system in a multicellular organism functions most like the cytoplasm in a single- celled organism?', choices: ['immune', 'nervous', 'reproductive', 'circulatory Living Environment–Aug. \'18'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 3, explanation: 'The cytoplasm of a single-celled organism transports materials between organelles throughout the cell — just as the circulatory system transports materials (oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste) throughout the body of a multicellular organism.', diveDeep: 'This question tests the concept of increasing complexity: in single-celled organisms, cellular structures perform all life functions. The cytoplasm serves as the internal transport medium, distributing substances from the cell membrane to organelles and between organelles. In multicellular organisms, the circulatory system (blood, blood vessels, heart) serves the analogous function — distributing materials throughout the organism. The immune system defends against pathogens. The nervous system coordinates responses. The reproductive system produces gametes. On the Regents, cytoplasm analog = circulatory system is a comparison that tests your ability to match single-cell structures to multicellular organ system equivalents.' },
    { number: 16, part: 'A', text: 'A common cycle in biology is represented below. ADP ATP P P The ATP molecule above is commonly used to', choices: ['actively transport molecules in an organism', 'diffuse water across a membrane', 'move molecules from a high to a low concentration', 'balance the nutrients in an ecosystem'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/q16.png', explanation: 'ATP provides the energy for active transport — the movement of molecules against their concentration gradient (from low to high concentration) across cell membranes, which requires energy expenditure.', diveDeep: 'ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the universal energy currency of cells. When ATP is hydrolyzed to ADP + phosphate, energy is released and used to power cellular work. Active transport uses this energy to pump ions and molecules across membranes against concentration gradients — for example, the sodium-potassium pump in neurons. Diffusion (choices B and C) is passive — it does not require energy and does not use ATP. Balancing ecosystem nutrients involves nutrient cycling by decomposers and organisms — not a cellular ATP function (choice D). On the Regents, ATP = energy for cellular work, especially active transport. Passive diffusion = no ATP required.' },
    { number: 17, part: 'A', text: 'Fat molecules typically contain long chains of carbon atoms. Animals tend to store fats for use when food resources are scarce. This is an advantage to the animal because', choices: ['much energy can be gained by breaking the bonds between atoms in the fats', 'fats give off carbon dioxide that can be used by the muscles', 'amino acids from fat synthesis are more easily digested than carbohydrates', 'energy can only be created by digesting fats'], topic: 'General', correct: 0, explanation: 'Fats have more C-H bonds per molecule than carbohydrates, meaning more energy is released when these bonds are broken during cellular respiration — fats are a more energy-dense storage molecule than glycogen.', diveDeep: 'Lipids (fats) contain about 9 calories per gram, compared to 4 calories per gram for carbohydrates and proteins. The long carbon chains in fatty acids contain many high-energy C-H bonds. When metabolized, these bonds release more energy than the C-O and C-OH bonds in sugars. This is why fat is an efficient long-term energy storage molecule. CO₂ is a waste product of respiration — muscles do not use CO₂ for energy (choice B is wrong). Fat synthesis does not produce amino acids — amino acids are building blocks of proteins (choice C confuses macromolecule categories). Energy can also be created by digesting carbohydrates and proteins (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, fats as energy storage = more C-H bonds = more energy per gram than carbohydrates.' },
    { number: 18, part: 'A', text: 'While looking at the bottom surface of a leaf with a compound light microscope, a student notices pairs of cells with openings between them on the surface of the leaf. The main purpose of these openings and the cells that surround them is', choices: ['removing excess sugars', 'synthesis of carbon dioxide', 'regulating gas exchange', 'puriﬁcation of water'], topic: 'General', correct: 2, explanation: 'The openings are stomata and the surrounding cells are guard cells. Their main function is to regulate gas exchange — controlling the entry of CO₂ for photosynthesis and the exit of O₂ and water vapor.', diveDeep: 'Stomata are pores on leaf surfaces (mainly the underside/lower epidermis) flanked by two guard cells. When guard cells swell with water (turgid), they open the stomata to allow CO₂ in (for photosynthesis) and O₂ and water vapor out (transpiration). When guard cells lose water (flaccid), they close the stomata to conserve water. Sugars are produced in the mesophyll cells and transported in the phloem — not removed through stomata. CO₂ is absorbed, not synthesized in leaves. Water purification is not a leaf function. On the Regents, stomata/guard cells = gas exchange regulation and water loss control. This is a fundamental plant biology concept.' },
    { number: 19, part: 'A', text: 'An immune response to a usually harmless environmental substance is known as', choices: ['an antigen', 'an allergy', 'a vaccination', 'a mutation'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 2 is "a vaccination," but a vaccination is an intentional immune preparation, not an overreaction to a harmless substance. An allergic reaction (index 1) is an immune overreaction to a normally harmless substance. An allergy is an immune system overreaction to a normally harmless environmental substance (like pollen, pet dander, or certain foods) — the immune system mistakenly treats it as a dangerous foreign antigen.', diveDeep: 'Allergic reactions involve IgE antibodies that sensitize mast cells to a specific allergen. Upon re-exposure, these mast cells release histamine and other chemicals causing symptoms like sneezing, itching, and in severe cases, anaphylaxis (breathing difficulty). An antigen is any substance that triggers an immune response (not specifically a harmless one). A vaccination introduces a weakened or killed pathogen to build immunity. A mutation is a change in DNA sequence. On the Regents, allergy = immune overreaction to harmless substance. Know the difference: antigen (anything triggering immune response), antibody (protein fighting antigen), allergy (overreaction to harmless substance), vaccination (deliberate immune preparation).' },
    { number: 20, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists at Penn State have sequenced the DNA of the extinct woolly mammoth. The data suggested that the woolly mammoth was more closely related to present-day elephants than previously believed. Elephant Woolly mammoth Which statement could account for the similarities between the woolly mammoth and present-day elephants?', choices: ['Common gene mutations were caused by agents such as industrial chemicals and radiation.', 'Present-day species developed from earlier, different species.', 'Selective breeding results in offspring better able to survive.', 'Both animals have identical genetic information.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'The DNA similarity between woolly mammoths and elephants indicates they evolved from a common ancestor — modern elephants developed from earlier species that they shared with mammoths through divergent evolution.', diveDeep: 'Comparative DNA analysis is one of the strongest forms of evidence for evolutionary relationships. The greater the DNA similarity between species, the more recently they shared a common ancestor. Woolly mammoths and modern elephants (African and Asian) all belong to the family Elephantidae, sharing a common ancestor from about 6 million years ago. Industrial chemicals and radiation did not cause the shared DNA similarities (choice A invents a cause). Selective breeding is a human-directed process — it did not produce wild mammoths or elephants (choice C). Mammoths and elephants do NOT have identical genetic information — they are different species (choice D). On the Regents, DNA/molecular similarity between species = common ancestry = evolution from shared ancestors.' },
    { number: 21, part: 'A', text: 'Single-celled organisms are able to maintain homeostasis, even though they lack higher levels of organization such as organs and organ systems, because', choices: ['single-celled organisms do not carry out the same life processes as multicellular organisms', 'multicellular organisms do not rely on tissues or organs to carry out life processes', 'cell structures work together to maintain homeostasis in single-celled organisms', 'single-celled organisms are able to coordinate organ functions to maintain homeostasis'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 2, explanation: 'In single-celled organisms, organelles (cell structures) collectively perform all life functions, working together to maintain homeostasis — the equivalent of organ systems in multicellular organisms.', diveDeep: 'Single-celled organisms (like Paramecium, Amoeba, or bacteria) perform the same basic life functions as multicellular organisms: nutrition, respiration, excretion, reproduction, and homeostasis. They accomplish this through the coordinated action of their cellular organelles. For example: contractile vacuoles regulate water balance; mitochondria produce energy; cell membranes control what enters/exits. Choice A is wrong — single-celled organisms DO carry out the same basic life processes. Choice B is wrong — multicellular organisms absolutely DO rely on tissues and organs. Choice D is wrong — single-celled organisms do not have organs to coordinate. On the Regents, organelles in unicellular organisms = organ systems in multicellular organisms. This is the increasing complexity comparison.' },
    { number: 22, part: 'A', text: 'Sharks are often followed by smaller ﬁsh that eat some of the scraps from the organisms eaten by the shark. These smaller ﬁsh are acting as', choices: ['decomposers', 'producers', 'scavengers', 'herbivores Living Environment–Aug. \'18'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 1 is "producers," but small fish eating scraps are clearly consumers, not producers. Scavengers (index 2) eat the remains/scraps of dead or killed organisms — that exactly matches fish eating scraps from the shark\'s meal. These fish are scavengers — organisms that consume the remains and scraps of organisms killed by other predators. They benefit from the shark\'s hunting without doing the killing themselves.', diveDeep: 'Scavengers occupy an important ecological role by consuming dead organic matter and scraps that would otherwise decompose. Well-known scavengers include vultures, hyenas, and various fish species that clean up after predators. This is different from decomposers (bacteria/fungi that break down matter at the molecular level) and different from herbivores (which eat plants). Producers make their own food via photosynthesis. On the Regents, organisms that eat leftover scraps/carcasses from other predators = scavengers. Distinguish: scavengers (eat dead animal matter) vs. decomposers (break down at the molecular level) vs. herbivores (eat plants).' },
    { number: 23, part: 'A', text: 'The photographs below are side-by-side images of twins A and B with identical genetic information. Twin A is a nonsmoker, while twin B is a longtime smoker. A B Source: http://holykaw.alltop.com The best explanation for the differences in the appearance of the twins is that', choices: ['twin B is older than twin A', 'they each inherited half of their DNA from each parent', 'the expression of genes is inﬂuenced by the environment', 'one twin resembles the mother and the other resembles the father'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 2, explanation: 'Identical twins have the same DNA, so differences in their appearance must be caused by environmental factors influencing how their genes are expressed — in this case, smoking alters gene expression and accelerates physical aging.', diveDeep: 'Environmental influences on gene expression (epigenetics) explain why identical twins (who share 100% of their DNA) can differ in appearance, health, and disease risk over time. Tobacco smoke introduces carcinogens and reactive oxygen species that cause epigenetic changes (DNA methylation, histone modification) and cellular damage that affect skin aging, wrinkling, and overall appearance. Identical twins are born at the same time from the same egg — twin B cannot be "older" (choice A is wrong). Both inherited the same DNA from both parents (choice B describes normal inheritance but doesn\'t explain the difference). They have identical genetics, so resembling different parents is impossible (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, identical twins with different traits = environment influencing gene expression.' },
    { number: 24, part: 'A', text: 'Under the supervision of experts, certain areas in a nature preserve are regularly exposed to frequent, low-intensity ﬁres. These controlled ﬁres maintain speciﬁc populations of plants by directly', choices: ['increasing the consumption of ﬁnite resources', 'decreasing the carbon dioxide level in the atmosphere', 'stopping the process of evolution', 'interfering with the process of ecological succession 25 An invasive species, the spiny water ﬂea, was recently found in a New York lake. These water ﬂeas eat zooplankton, a food also consumed by native ﬁshes. The ﬂeas spread from lake to lake by attaching to ﬁshing lines, anchor ropes, and boats. Which statement best describes the effect of the water ﬂea on the lake?'], topic: 'General', correct: 3, explanation: 'Controlled burns interrupt the natural process of ecological succession by removing trees and shrubs that would eventually dominate the area, maintaining earlier successional stages with specific plant communities (like fire-adapted grasses and shrubs).', diveDeep: 'Ecological succession is the natural progression of an ecosystem from pioneer species toward a climax community (e.g., grassland → shrubland → forest). By periodically burning areas, managers reset the successional clock, preventing the establishment of late-successional species and maintaining the early-stage plant communities that many fire-adapted species depend on. This is a management technique used in longleaf pine forests, chaparral, and African savanna ecosystems. Controlled burns consume some resources but are managed to minimize resource loss (choice A is not the purpose). Fires release CO₂ (choice B is wrong — they increase atmospheric CO₂ temporarily). Evolution cannot be stopped by fires (choice C). On the Regents, controlled burns = interfering with/preventing ecological succession.' },
    { number: 25, part: 'A', text: 'An invasive species, the spiny water flea, was recently found in a New York lake. These water fleas eat zooplankton, a food also consumed by native fishes. Which statement best describes the most likely effect of the water flea on the native fish populations in the lake?', choices: ['Native fish populations will increase because they now have a new food source.', 'Native fish populations will remain the same because zooplankton is abundant.', 'Native fish populations will likely decrease due to reduced food supply from increased competition.', 'Native fish populations will migrate to other lakes to find food.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 2, explanation: 'The spiny water flea competes directly with native fish for zooplankton. As water fleas consume more zooplankton, less food is available for native fish, leading to decreased native fish populations due to food competition.', diveDeep: 'Invasive species disrupt ecosystems by competing with native species for the same limited resources (competitive exclusion principle). The spiny water flea (Bythotrephes longimanus) is a real invasive species in the Great Lakes and New York waterways — it eats zooplankton such as Daphnia that are critical food for young fish. Reduced zooplankton means less food for native fish, causing population declines. Native fish cannot simply gain the water flea as a food source in significant enough quantities to offset their loss of zooplankton (choice A). Zooplankton populations themselves decline under heavy predation by water fleas, so choice B is unrealistic. Fish migration is an unlikely first response (choice D). On the Regents, invasive species competing for the same food = decreased native populations through competition.' },
    { number: 26, part: 'A', text: 'The nervous system and endocrine system work together in multicellular organisms primarily to', choices: ['produce genetic variations in offspring', 'digest food and absorb nutrients', 'maintain stable internal conditions (homeostasis)', 'transport oxygen to all body cells'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, explanation: 'The nervous system (rapid electrical signals) and endocrine system (slower chemical hormones) work together to detect changes in the internal and external environment and respond to keep internal conditions stable — maintaining homeostasis.', diveDeep: 'Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment despite external changes. The nervous system detects stimuli via receptors and responds rapidly through nerve impulses. The endocrine system releases hormones (chemical messengers) that travel through blood for slower, longer-lasting regulation. Together, they regulate body temperature, blood glucose, blood pressure, water balance, and many other internal variables. Classic examples: blood glucose regulation (insulin/glucagon from pancreas controlled by feedback involving the nervous system); body temperature (hypothalamus nervous system triggers shivering, sweat glands). On the Regents, nervous system + endocrine system = coordination and homeostasis. Distinguish: nervous system = fast/electrical; endocrine = slow/chemical.' },
    { number: 27, part: 'A', text: 'A student uses a compound light microscope to observe cells from the tip of an onion root. The student notices that some cells appear to have two complete sets of chromosomes being pulled to opposite ends of the cell. Which process is the student most likely observing?', choices: ['meiosis, which produces four genetically diverse cells', 'mitosis, which produces two genetically identical cells', 'fertilization, which combines gametes to form a zygote', 'differentiation, which produces specialized cell types'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Chromosomes being pulled to opposite ends of a dividing cell is the hallmark of mitosis (specifically anaphase). Mitosis produces two daughter cells with the same genetic information as the parent cell — genetically identical cells used for growth and repair.', diveDeep: 'Onion root tips are a classic microscopy preparation for observing mitosis because cells at the root tip divide rapidly. During mitosis, chromosomes condense (prophase), align at the center (metaphase), are pulled to opposite poles by spindle fibers (anaphase), and then the cell divides (telophase/cytokinesis). The result is two genetically identical diploid cells. Meiosis occurs in reproductive organs, produces four haploid cells (gametes), and involves genetic recombination — not occurring in onion roots. Fertilization combines gametes — not observable in a root tip. Differentiation is a developmental process, not a division type. On the Regents, chromosomes pulled apart in body cells = mitosis = two identical daughter cells.' },
    { number: 28, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists inserted the human insulin gene into bacterial DNA, causing the bacteria to produce human insulin. The technique used to insert the gene into the bacterial DNA is known as', choices: ['selective breeding', 'recombinant DNA technology', 'chromatography', 'natural selection'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 1, explanation: 'Inserting a gene from one organism (human insulin gene) into another organism\'s DNA (bacterial plasmid) to produce a desired protein is the definition of recombinant DNA technology — combining DNA from two different sources.', diveDeep: 'Recombinant DNA technology (genetic engineering) involves: (1) using restriction enzymes to cut the human insulin gene and bacterial plasmid at specific sequences; (2) using DNA ligase to insert the human gene into the plasmid; (3) inserting the recombinant plasmid into bacteria; (4) bacteria express the human gene and produce human insulin. This process is used to produce insulin (for diabetics), human growth hormone, vaccines, and many other medical products. Selective breeding uses natural reproduction over many generations — it cannot insert specific genes (choice A). Chromatography separates molecules — it does not manipulate genes (choice C). Natural selection is an evolutionary mechanism, not a lab technique (choice D). On the Regents, inserting genes from one organism into another = recombinant DNA technology / genetic engineering.' },
    { number: 29, part: 'A', text: 'During aerobic respiration in a cell, glucose is broken down and energy is released. In which organelle does this process primarily occur?', choices: ['ribosome', 'chloroplast', 'nucleus', 'mitochondria'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 3, explanation: 'Aerobic respiration — the process of breaking down glucose using oxygen to release ATP energy — takes place primarily in the mitochondria. This is why mitochondria are called the "powerhouse of the cell."', diveDeep: 'Aerobic respiration involves three main stages: (1) glycolysis in the cytoplasm (glucose → pyruvate, small ATP yield); (2) Krebs cycle in the mitochondrial matrix; (3) electron transport chain on the inner mitochondrial membrane (largest ATP yield). The overall reaction: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ~38 ATP. Ribosomes synthesize proteins (choice A). Chloroplasts perform photosynthesis — the opposite process, building glucose from CO₂ using light energy (choice B). The nucleus contains DNA and directs gene expression (choice C). On the Regents, aerobic respiration = mitochondria = ATP production. Remember the contrast: chloroplast = photosynthesis (makes glucose), mitochondria = respiration (breaks down glucose).' },
    { number: 30, part: 'A', text: 'Why is biodiversity important in an ecosystem?', choices: ['A greater variety of species reduces the amount of energy available in the ecosystem.', 'Ecosystems with fewer species are more productive than those with many species.', 'A greater variety of species increases ecosystem stability and resilience to environmental change.', 'Biodiversity has no measurable effect on ecosystem function.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 2, explanation: 'Biodiversity (variety of species) increases ecosystem stability because a more diverse ecosystem has more redundancy — if one species is lost, others can fill similar roles. Diverse ecosystems are more resilient to disturbances like disease, drought, and invasive species.', diveDeep: 'Ecosystems with high biodiversity have multiple species occupying similar ecological roles (functional redundancy), meaning that the loss of one species can be compensated by others. This makes the ecosystem more stable and resistant to disturbances. In contrast, low-biodiversity ecosystems (monocultures) are highly vulnerable — a single disease or pest can destroy the entire system (e.g., the Irish Potato Famine). Biodiversity also supports ecosystem services like pollination, water purification, and nutrient cycling. Greater variety does not reduce energy — it usually increases total biomass and energy flow (choice A is wrong). Low-diversity monocultures may have high short-term yield but are fragile (choice B is wrong). Biodiversity has significant measurable effects on ecosystem function (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, biodiversity = stability and resilience.' },
    { number: 31, part: 'B-1', text: 'A student graphed enzyme activity versus temperature for a human digestive enzyme. The graph showed enzyme activity increasing from 20°C to a peak at 37°C, then dropping sharply above 40°C. What best explains why enzyme activity decreases at temperatures above 40°C?', choices: ['The enzyme runs out of substrate molecules to react with.', 'The enzyme becomes denatured — its shape changes and it can no longer bind to the substrate.', 'The enzyme becomes more active and works too fast, consuming all products.', 'Lower pH at high temperatures inactivates the enzyme.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'At temperatures above the optimal (37°C for human enzymes), the high heat disrupts the weak bonds (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds) that maintain the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape. The active site is deformed — the enzyme is denatured — and can no longer bind substrate.', diveDeep: 'Enzymes are proteins whose function depends on their precise three-dimensional shape (tertiary structure). The active site must have the exact shape to bind the specific substrate (lock-and-key or induced fit model). At high temperatures, thermal energy breaks the weak bonds maintaining the protein\'s shape. Denaturation is typically irreversible — the enzyme permanently loses its function. Human enzymes are optimized for body temperature (37°C). Fever above 41°C can denature important enzymes, which is life-threatening. The enzyme does not run out of substrate — substrate concentration is a separate variable (choice A). Temperature denaturation is not pH-related (choice D). On the Regents, enzyme inactivation at high temperature = denaturation = changed shape = inactive active site.' },
    { number: 32, part: 'B-1', text: 'A food web shows: Grass → Rabbits → Foxes → Hawks. If the rabbit population suddenly decreases significantly, which population would most directly increase as a result?', choices: ['Hawks, because they would switch to eating more foxes', 'Foxes, because they would reproduce faster without competition', 'Grass, because less of it would be consumed by rabbits', 'Hawks, because less competition with foxes means more food'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 2, explanation: 'Rabbits eat grass. If the rabbit population decreases, less grass is consumed, so the grass population would increase (reduced predation pressure on grass). This is a trophic cascade effect moving down the food chain.', diveDeep: 'In a food chain, removing or reducing a consumer causes an increase in its prey population (bottom-up effect). With fewer rabbits grazing on grass, grass grows more abundantly — this is called a trophic cascade. Meanwhile, fox and hawk populations would likely decrease due to reduced food supply (fewer rabbits to eat). Foxes do not benefit from fewer rabbits — they would starve (choice B is wrong). Hawks eat foxes, but if foxes decrease (from fewer rabbits), hawk food also decreases (choices A and D are wrong). On the Regents, food web removal questions: always trace the direct connection. Reducing a consumer = prey population increases. Reducing a producer = consumer population decreases.' },
    { number: 33, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows a DNA molecule being replicated. The template strand has the sequence A-T-G-C. Which sequence would be found on the newly synthesized complementary strand?', choices: ['A-T-G-C', 'T-A-C-G', 'U-A-C-G', 'G-C-T-A'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/context_33_34.png', explanation: 'During DNA replication, each base pairs with its complementary base: A pairs with T, T pairs with A, G pairs with C, C pairs with G. The complement of A-T-G-C is T-A-C-G.', diveDeep: 'DNA replication follows the base-pairing rules (Chargaff\'s rules): Adenine (A) always pairs with Thymine (T); Guanine (G) always pairs with Cytosine (C). The new strand is antiparallel and complementary to the template. So for template A-T-G-C, the new strand is T-A-C-G. Note: U (uracil) replaces T only in RNA, not in DNA replication (eliminate choice C). The complement of G-C would be C-G, not the reverse (choice D misapplies the rule). On the Regents, DNA complementary base pairing: A↔T, G↔C. For RNA transcription: A↔U, T↔A, G↔C, C↔G. Uracil (U) only appears in RNA, never in DNA.' },
    { number: 34, part: 'B-1', text: 'A pedigree chart shows three generations of a family. In the second generation, two unaffected parents have one affected child and two unaffected children. What inheritance pattern does this pedigree most likely suggest?', choices: ['autosomal dominant — the trait appears in every generation', 'X-linked recessive — only males are affected', 'autosomal recessive — both parents carry a hidden allele that can produce an affected child', 'codominant — both alleles are expressed equally'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/context_33_34.png', explanation: 'Two unaffected (carrier) parents producing an affected child is the hallmark of autosomal recessive inheritance — both parents carry one recessive allele (Aa) but are phenotypically normal; each child has a 25% chance of inheriting both recessive alleles (aa) and being affected.', diveDeep: 'In autosomal recessive inheritance, the allele causing the trait is recessive (a). Carriers (Aa) are phenotypically normal but can pass the recessive allele to offspring. When two carriers mate (Aa × Aa), offspring ratios are: 25% AA (unaffected, non-carrier), 50% Aa (unaffected carriers), 25% aa (affected). This explains why two unaffected parents can have an affected child. Autosomal dominant traits (choice A) would show an affected parent in the pedigree. X-linked recessive (choice B) typically shows more affected males than females. Codominant traits (choice D) show blended or both phenotypes, not skip-generation patterns. On the Regents, two unaffected parents → affected child = autosomal recessive. Always the most tested pedigree pattern.' },
    { number: 35, part: 'B-1', text: 'A graph shows the population of deer in a forest over 50 years. The population grows rapidly at first, then levels off and fluctuates around a constant number. What factor most likely causes the population to stop growing and level off?', choices: ['The deer develop a genetic mutation that reduces reproduction.', 'The deer population reaches the carrying capacity of the environment due to limited resources.', 'All predators of deer are removed from the ecosystem.', 'The deer migrate to a new habitat with more resources.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, explanation: 'The S-shaped (logistic) growth curve levels off when the population reaches the carrying capacity — the maximum population size an environment can sustainably support given limited food, space, water, and other resources.', diveDeep: 'Logistic population growth has two phases: (1) exponential growth (J-shaped portion) when resources are abundant and birth rate greatly exceeds death rate; (2) leveling off as the population approaches carrying capacity (K), when limited resources (food, territory, water, nesting sites) cause death rate to equal birth rate. At carrying capacity, the population fluctuates around K due to environmental variation. Genetic mutations do not cause this predictable pattern (choice A). Removing predators would cause the population to exceed K and then crash, not level off smoothly (choice C). Migration away from the habitat would decrease population, not cause leveling off (choice D). On the Regents, S-curve / population leveling off = carrying capacity = limited resources.' },
    { number: 36, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram of a cell shows organelles labeled A through D. Organelle B is a double-membraned structure containing its own DNA and internal membrane stacks with green pigment. Which statement about organelle B is correct?', choices: ['Organelle B is the mitochondria, found in both plant and animal cells.', 'Organelle B is the chloroplast, found only in plant and algae cells.', 'Organelle B is the nucleus, which controls all cell activities.', 'Organelle B is the ribosome, which synthesizes proteins.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/q36.png', explanation: 'A double-membraned organelle with its own DNA and internal membrane stacks (thylakoids) containing green pigment (chlorophyll) is definitively the chloroplast — found only in plant cells and algae, used for photosynthesis.', diveDeep: 'Chloroplasts are organelles unique to plant cells and algae that perform photosynthesis. Key features: double membrane (outer and inner envelope), internal membrane stacks called thylakoids (organized into grana), contain chlorophyll (green pigment that captures light energy), contain their own circular DNA and ribosomes (evidence for endosymbiotic origin). Mitochondria are also double-membraned with their own DNA, but are found in both plant AND animal cells, and lack green pigment (choice A). The nucleus is a single large organelle that controls gene expression — it does not have thylakoid stacks or green pigment (choice C). Ribosomes are tiny, not double-membraned (choice D). On the Regents, chloroplasts = green, plant only, photosynthesis. Mitochondria = both plant and animal, respiration.' },
    { number: 37, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/context_37_39.png', part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows a population of rabbits with varying fur colors (light and dark) in a snowy environment. Over many generations, the proportion of dark-furred rabbits decreases while light-furred rabbits increase. What best explains this change in the population?', choices: ['Dark-furred rabbits chose to move to warmer climates over time.', 'Light-furred rabbits are better camouflaged in snow, allowing them to survive longer and reproduce more.', 'Dark-furred rabbits spontaneously changed their fur color to white.', 'All rabbits gradually developed white fur through use and disuse.'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, explanation: 'In a snowy environment, light-furred rabbits are better camouflaged — predators have difficulty spotting them, so more light-furred rabbits survive to reproduce. This differential survival and reproduction based on an inherited trait (fur color) is natural selection.', diveDeep: 'Natural selection acts on heritable variation in a population: individuals with traits better suited to the environment survive and reproduce more (differential reproductive success). Their advantageous traits (encoded in genes) are passed to more offspring. Over generations, the frequency of the advantageous allele (light fur in snowy environment) increases in the population. This is evolution by natural selection. Individual animals do not "choose" to move based on their fur color (choice A — this is not how evolution works). Individual organisms do not change their DNA during their lifetime (choice C). Lamarck\'s idea of use-and-disuse (choice D) was disproved — traits acquired during life are not inherited. On the Regents, camouflage example = natural selection = better adapted individuals survive and reproduce more.' },
    { number: 38, part: 'B-1', text: 'A student designed an experiment to test the effect of fertilizer concentration on plant growth. She set up four groups of identical bean plants: Group 1 received no fertilizer, Group 2 received low-concentration fertilizer, Group 3 received medium-concentration fertilizer, and Group 4 received high-concentration fertilizer. All other conditions were identical. What is the control group in this experiment?', choices: ['Group 4, because it has the highest fertilizer concentration', 'Group 2, because it has the lowest amount of fertilizer applied', 'Group 1, because it received no fertilizer and represents the baseline condition', 'There is no control group because all plants received the same water and light'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 2, explanation: 'The control group is Group 1 — plants that received no fertilizer. The control group provides a baseline (normal conditions without the experimental variable) against which the experimental groups can be compared to determine the effect of fertilizer.', diveDeep: 'In a controlled experiment: the independent variable is what the experimenter changes (fertilizer concentration); the dependent variable is what is measured (plant growth); the control group is the group that does NOT receive the independent variable — it provides a baseline for comparison. The control group must be treated identically to experimental groups in every way EXCEPT for the independent variable. Without a control group, there is no way to know if changes in plant growth are caused by fertilizer or by other uncontrolled factors. Group 4 (most fertilizer) is an experimental group, not the control (choice A). Group 2 has some fertilizer, so it is still an experimental group (choice B). All groups do have the same water and light, but Group 1 (no fertilizer) is still the control (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, control group = no independent variable applied = baseline comparison.' },
    { number: 39, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows the human immune response to a bacterial infection. During the first exposure, the immune system produces antibodies and also forms memory cells. What is the primary benefit of these memory cells?', choices: ['Memory cells immediately destroy the pathogen during the first infection.', 'Memory cells produce hormones that prevent future infections of any kind.', 'Memory cells allow a faster and stronger immune response upon re-exposure to the same pathogen.', 'Memory cells replace damaged tissue after an infection clears.'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/context_37_39.png', explanation: 'Memory cells (memory B-cells and memory T-cells) persist long after the initial infection is cleared. Upon re-exposure to the same antigen, these memory cells rapidly recognize it and mount a faster, stronger secondary immune response — often eliminating the pathogen before symptoms appear.', diveDeep: 'The primary immune response (first exposure) takes 1–2 weeks to produce enough antibodies — during this time the person feels sick. Memory B-cells and memory T-cells are long-lived cells produced during this response that "remember" the specific antigen. The secondary immune response (re-exposure) is much faster (1–3 days) and produces more antibodies (higher titer) due to memory cells. This is the basis for vaccination — vaccines introduce a harmless antigen to create memory cells without causing disease, so the body is prepared for real infection. Memory cells do not act during the first exposure (choice A). Memory cells are antigen-specific — they don\'t provide general immunity to all pathogens (choice B). Memory cells are immune cells, not tissue repair cells (choice D). On the Regents, memory cells = faster/stronger secondary immune response = basis of vaccination.' },
    { number: 40, part: 'B-1', text: 'A graph shows the population sizes of wolves and deer in a national park over 30 years. The deer population peaks, then the wolf population peaks shortly after, then the deer population declines. When the deer population is at its highest point, what would most likely happen to the wolf population in the following years?', choices: ['The wolf population would immediately decrease due to overhunting of deer.', 'The wolf population would increase because more deer means more food available for wolves.', 'The wolf population would remain unchanged regardless of deer abundance.', 'The wolf population would decrease because wolves prefer to eat other prey.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/q40.png', explanation: 'When the deer (prey) population is at its highest, food is abundant for wolves (predators). With more food available, wolves survive better and reproduce more, so the wolf population increases. This is the classic predator-prey cycle.', diveDeep: 'Predator-prey relationships create cyclical population oscillations (Lotka-Volterra model): (1) Prey population increases → more food for predators → predator population increases. (2) More predators eat more prey → prey population decreases. (3) Less food → predator population decreases. (4) With fewer predators, prey population recovers → cycle repeats. These cycles are slightly offset — the predator peak follows the prey peak by a time delay. This pattern is observed in real ecosystems (Canadian lynx and snowshoe hare data is a classic example). Wolves do not overhunt immediately — the increase in wolf population takes time (choice A conflates short-term and long-term). Population changes are not independent of food supply (choice C). Wolves are adapted to hunt deer as primary prey (choice D). On the Regents, more prey = more predator food = predator population increases (time-delayed).' },
    { number: 41, part: 'B-1', text: 'A researcher compares the DNA sequences of three bird species. Species A and Species B share 95% of their DNA sequence. Species A and Species C share only 60% of their DNA sequence. Which conclusion is best supported by this data?', choices: ['Species B and Species C are more closely related to each other than either is to Species A.', 'Species A and Species B are more closely related to each other than either is to Species C.', 'All three species evolved from Species C because it has the most different DNA.', 'DNA sequence similarity has no relationship to evolutionary relatedness.'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, explanation: 'Species A and B share 95% of their DNA — a high degree of similarity indicating they diverged from a common ancestor more recently. Species A and C share only 60%, indicating a more distant evolutionary relationship. Greater DNA similarity = more closely related.', diveDeep: 'Molecular phylogenetics uses DNA (and protein) sequence comparisons to determine evolutionary relationships. The principle: the more similar the DNA sequences, the more recently two species shared a common ancestor (less time for mutations to accumulate). This is analogous to comparing a document that has been copied and changed over time — the more different copies are, the more editing (mutation) has occurred. 95% DNA similarity between A and B means very few mutations separate them = recent common ancestor. 60% similarity between A and C = many more mutations = older common ancestor. This data says nothing about B vs. C relationship (choice A requires B-C comparison data). Species C\'s differences from A do not mean it is ancestral to A and B (choice C). DNA similarity is one of the most reliable indicators of evolutionary relatedness (choice D). On the Regents, more DNA similarity = more closely related = more recent common ancestor.' },
    { number: 42, part: 'B-1', text: 'A student set up an experiment with two groups of identical seedlings. Group 1 received full light exposure for 12 hours per day. Group 2 received no light. Both groups received identical amounts of water, the same soil, and were kept at the same temperature. What is the independent variable in this experiment?', choices: ['the amount of water each group of seedlings received', 'the type of soil used for both groups of seedlings', 'the light exposure received by each group of seedlings', 'the growth rate of the seedlings over time'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 2, explanation: 'The independent variable is the factor that the experimenter deliberately changes between groups. In this experiment, the only factor that differs between Group 1 and Group 2 is the light exposure (full light vs. no light) — making light exposure the independent variable.', diveDeep: 'In experimental design: the independent variable (IV) is what is deliberately manipulated by the experimenter; the dependent variable (DV) is what is measured as a result; controlled variables are all other factors kept the same to ensure a fair test. Here: IV = light exposure (changed: full light vs. no light); DV = seedling growth rate; controlled variables = water amount, soil type, temperature. Water is a controlled variable — it is kept identical in both groups (choice A). Soil type is also a controlled variable (choice B). Growth rate is the dependent variable — what is being measured (choice D). On the Regents, independent variable = what the scientist changes on purpose. Dependent variable = what is measured. Control = what is kept the same.' },
    { number: 43, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows a cell membrane with pure water on the left side and a high-concentration sugar solution on the right side. Over time, water molecules move from the left side (low solute concentration) to the right side (high solute concentration) through the membrane. What process does this diagram illustrate?', choices: ['active transport, which requires ATP to move water against the concentration gradient', 'diffusion of sugar molecules from high to low concentration', 'osmosis, which is the diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane from low solute to high solute concentration', 'endocytosis, where the cell membrane engulfs water molecules'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-august-2018/q43.png', explanation: 'Water moving across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of low solute concentration (high water concentration) to a region of high solute concentration (low water concentration) is osmosis — a type of passive diffusion specific to water molecules.', diveDeep: 'Osmosis is a specific type of diffusion: the movement of water molecules across a selectively permeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration (higher water concentration) to an area of higher solute concentration (lower water concentration). This is passive — no ATP is required. Remember: water moves toward where there is more dissolved solute (high solute = low water concentration). This is critical for understanding: plant cell turgor pressure (water enters via osmosis keeping cells firm), animal cell crenation (water exits cells in high-salt solution), and IV fluid chemistry in medicine. Active transport requires ATP and moves solutes (not water) against gradients (choice A). Diffusion of sugar would move sugar, not water (choice B). Endocytosis engulfs large particles, not individual water molecules (choice D). On the Regents, osmosis = water movement through membrane from low to high solute concentration = passive (no ATP).' },
  ]
}
